// or https://\', \'type\': \'str\'}, \'css_element\': {\'description\': \'Mandatory css reference for element to scrape from the website\', \'type\': \'str\'}}\nTool Description: A tool that can be used to read a website content.\n\nIMPORTANT: Use the following format in your response:\n\n```\nThought: you should always think about what to do\nAction: the action to take, only one name of [Code Interpreter, Read a website content], just the name, exactly as it\'s written.\nAction Input: the input to the action, just a simple JSON object, enclosed in curly braces, using " to wrap keys and values.\nObservation: the result of the action\n```\n\nOnce all necessary information is gathered, return the following format:\n\n```\nThought: I now know the final answer\nFinal Answer: the final answer to the original input question\n```'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nCurrent Task: Using the list of urls from the source_scout agent as your ONLY input, evaluate each url following the workflow shown below.\nGOAL: Process each and every url.  For each url provided,use the url metadata and the source information to to evaluate and score it on: - relevance,  - credibility - recency In addition you should test the accessibility of each URL.  You will process each URL individually to ensure that you have evaluated and scored all sources.\n**MANDATORY WORKFLOW PER SOURCE:** 1. For each url you will score it between 1 and 10, where 1 is the lowest and 10 is the highest:\n  - Relevance to Global Market Forces affecting management consulting  (1-10) - score the url lower if it links to a overview site with no significant content\n  - Credibility of source/author (1-10)\n  - Recency (1-10, with a high priority for sources published more recently, score 0 for any sources published more than 1 year ago given the current date is 19 May 2025) example of recency rating:\n  - greater than 1 year old source: 0 \n  - greater than 6 months old source: 7\n  - greater than 3 months old source: 9\n  - less than 1 month old source: 10\n  \n2. Calculate a total score by taking the sum of the relevance, credibility and recency scores 3. Accessibility - Test if the url links to a valid website or PDF, if the site is valid inidicate it with "Yes" if not indicate it with "No" - eliminate any paywalled or overview sites with no significant content  4. In order to score the url you will need to:\n  - Determine if the url is a website or pdf, based on the source type.\n  - If the source type is "website":\n    1. Use a tool to read the website content for this url. Let the result be `Observation_HTML`.\n    2. **IMMEDIATE NEXT STEP:** Your *only* goal now is to analyze `Observation_HTML`.\n      Think: "I need to evaluate this url based on the HTML I just received."\n      Formulate an internal request to yourself like: "Evaluate this url based on this content: [Paste the *full* Observation_HTML here]. Score it on relevance (1-10), credibility (1-10), and recency (1-10)."\n      *You will internally process this request*. The result is `Evaluation_For_This_URL`.\n  - If the source type is a pdf:\n    1. Do NOT try to read the pdf content or open the pdf file\n    2. Instead, assess quality based on the url metadata only\n    3. Include the note "PDF document - quality assessed from metadata only" in any PDF descriptions\n\n**VERY IMPORTANT: Process each url ONE BY ONE to avoid context window limitations. Do not attempt to process all urls at once.**\n\n\nThis is the expected criteria for your final answer: A complete list of all evaluated urls with scoring for each url structured according to the EvaluatedSources model.\n\nyou MUST return the actual complete content as the final answer, not a summary.\nEnsure your final answer contains only the content in the following format: {\n  "evaluated_sources": List[{\n  "no": int,\n  "url": str,\n  "title": str,\n  "source_type": str,\n  "source_date": Optional[str],\n  "relevance_score": float,\n  "credibility_score": float,\n